{
  "reasoning": "The product is a tricyclic system consisting of an indole core fused to a saturated six-membered carbocycle bearing a cyano group. This architecture is classically accessed via the Fischer indole synthesis, a heterocycle formation reaction between a phenylhydrazine and a ketone. Retrosynthetic disconnection of the indole ring reveals the two key precursors: (1) phenylhydrazine, which provides the indole nitrogen and the fused benzene ring, represented as c1ccccc1NN; and (2) a cyclic ketone that supplies the fused carbocycle and the cyano substituent, identified as 2-cyanocyclohexanone (N#CC1CCCCC1=O). In the forward direction, condensation of these two components forms a hydrazone intermediate, which undergoes acid-catalyzed [3,2]-sigmatropic rearrangement followed by elimination of ammonia to yield the observed fused indole product.",
  "final_answer": "N#CC1CCCCC1=O.c1ccccc1NN"
}

